Abstract

We present a mathematical framework in which a genome is formalised as a constructor in the sense of constructor theory: a digital information medium that, together with a cellular vehicle, defines the subset of the universal space of physically possible organisms that it can bring into existence. The universal space \Omega is modelled as a metric space endowed with a reference dissimilarity measure; when smoothness is assumed locally, it carries a Riemannian metric g_\Omega. Development is a stochastic physical process, giving for each genome G a probability measure P_G on \Omega; its support is the constructibility space \Omega_G. The constructibility capacity \kappa(G) is defined as the effective number of distinguishable organisms G can produce. Genomes are compared via two complementary distances: the Fisher–Rao information distance on the statistical manifold of constructibility measures, and, when a common metric on \Omega exists, the Wasserstein‑2 distance between the measures. The Fisher information metric endows the space of genomes \mathcal{G} with a Riemannian metric h that emerges naturally from the covariance of allele frequencies—this is the Shahshahani metric. We ground evolutionary dynamics in the universal Force–Metric–Bias (FMB) law derived from the Price equation, showing that in the absence of bias and noise the mean genome follows a Riemannian gradient flow of expected fitness on (\mathcal{G}, h), thereby recovering the Lande equation and replicator dynamics. The framework yields precise geometric definitions of robustness, evolvability, and innovation, and addresses the thermodynamical cost of construction. We critically examine the regularity conditions, the role of epigenetic inheritance as an analogue constructor, the relationship to Waddington’s landscape and the adjacent possible, and propose concrete pathways to empirical validation. The theory stands as a candidate for the mathematical logic of genomic action, bridging constructor theory, information geometry, and covariant evolutionary dynamics.

