1. Introduction

Modern genetics has long abandoned the simplistic notion of a genome as a blueprint for an organism. Critics have thoroughly debunked the blueprint metaphor: it implies one‑to‑one mapping, deterministic specification, and reversibility that biological development does not exhibit (Fisher, 2018; Ainsworth, 2015; Pigliucci, 2017). In its place, several alternative conceptions have been offered—the genome as a developmental code, a generative model, or a reactive system (Mitchell, 2025; Quanta Magazine, 2026). What has been missing, however, is a rigorous mathematical instantiation of a post‑blueprint perspective that is anchored in fundamental physical law and that directly interfaces with the formal structure of evolutionary theory.

We propose that a genome is fundamentally a constructor of biological possibility. It does not encode a finished organism; rather, under the laws of physics and within a permissible environment, it specifies a subset of the universe of physically realisable organisms that can be brought into existence. This shift moves the primary object of study from phenotype prediction to the geometry of constructibility.

The framework we develop draws on three pillars:

1. Constructor theory (Deutsch, 2012; Deutsch & Marletto, 2014; Marletto, 2015), which reformulates physical laws as statements about which tasks are possible and which are impossible. In this setting, a genome is a digital information medium—a replicator—that, together with a cellular vehicle, accomplishes the task of converting a nutrient substrate into an organism while retaining the ability to perform the task again. This yields a precise, counterfactual definition of the constructibility space \Omega_G.
2. Information geometry and optimal transport (Amari, 2016; Villani, 2009), which provide a natural metric structure on spaces of probability measures. The family of constructibility measures \{P_G\}_{G\in\mathcal{G}} forms a statistical manifold; the Fisher information metric emerges as the unique Riemannian metric that contracts under physical transformations (Petz et al., 2023), and it coincides with the Shahshahani metric on genotype frequencies. When a common morphological distance exists, the Wasserstein distance supplies a complementary metric on the underlying phenotype space.
3. The covariant unification of evolutionary dynamics via the universal Force–Metric–Bias (FMB) law (Frank, 2025a,b), which shows that any adaptive process obeying the Price equation can be written as \Delta \bar{\theta} = \mathbf{M} f + \mathbf{b} + \boldsymbol{\xi}, where \mathbf{M} is a Riemannian metric (the genetic covariance), f is the selection gradient, \mathbf{b} is bias, and \boldsymbol{\xi} is noise. In the appropriate limit, evolution becomes a Riemannian gradient flow on the manifold of constructors.

The resulting theory is not a rejection of mathematical genetics but a geometric deepening of it. It provides precise, coordinate‑independent definitions of robustness, evolvability, evolutionary innovation, and the thermodynamic cost of construction. It connects naturally to Waddington’s epigenetic landscape, the adjacent possible, and epigenetic inheritance. We present the framework in full mathematical detail, critically examine its foundational assumptions, and chart a programme for empirical validation.

