2. Constructor‑Theoretic Foundations

2.1 Tasks, Constructors, and Possibility

Constructor theory (Deutsch, 2012) describes the world in terms of possible and impossible tasks, without assuming a designated initial state or a pre‑existing dynamics.

Definition 2.1 (Task). A task \mathcal{T} is a set of ordered pairs of input–output attributes of a substrate,

\mathcal{T} = \{ x_i \to y_i \}_{i\in I},

where x_i and y_i are legitimate states of the substrate. The task is possible if the laws of physics permit a constructor to perform it to arbitrarily high accuracy; otherwise it is impossible.

Definition 2.2 (Constructor). A constructor C for a possible task \mathcal{T} is an entity that, whenever presented with the substrate in an input state x_i\in\mathcal{T}, transforms it to the corresponding output state y_i while retaining the ability to perform the task again (resiliency).

The constructor theory of life (Marletto, 2015) proves that accurate self‑reproduction and natural selection are possible under no‑design laws, provided those laws allow digital information to be physically instantiated. Crucially, it establishes a modular decomposition: any self‑reproducing entity must contain a replicator (a digital information medium with resiliency) and a vehicle (the physical constructor that uses the replicator’s information to build a new vehicle and copy the replicator).

2.2 Genome as Constructor

We adopt this decomposition. Let S_0 be a standard nutrient substrate, and let E be an ensemble of admissible environmental conditions.

Definition 2.3 (Genome as Constructor). A genome G is a physical instantiation of digital information that acts as the replicator. The vehicle—comprising the cellular machinery, ribosomes, maternal factors, etc.—together with G constitutes a constructor for the task

\mathcal{T}_G = \big\{ \big( (e, S_0),\, \omega \big) \;\big|\; e \in E,\; \omega \in \Omega_{G,e} \big\},

where \Omega_{G,e} is the set of organisms that can be produced from G in environment e. The constructor takes the substrate (e, S_0) and transforms it into an organism \omega, resetting the replicator (by replication) in the process. The constructibility space of G is the union over all admissible environments:

\Omega_G := \bigcup_{e \in E} \Omega_{G,e} \;\subseteq\; \Omega,

where \Omega is the universal space of physically possible organisms (Section 3).

Thus a genome specifies, via the laws of physics and the vehicle’s structure, a well‑defined subset of biological possibility. The requirement that G be a digital information medium—copyable with high fidelity—guarantees the resiliency necessary for evolution by natural selection.

