3. The Universal Space of Biological Possibility \Omega

3.1 Existence and Structure

The laws of physics (quantum electrodynamics, thermodynamics, etc.) define a set of all macroscopic states that can be recognised as a living organism under given constraints (Earth‑like chemistry, bounded volume, finite energy). We denote this set \Omega. Its exact mathematical structure is unknown, but we can characterise it through successive approximation.

Definition 3.1 (Phenotypic Morphospace). Let organisms be described by a vector \mathbf{x} of N phenotypic traits (morphological, physiological, behavioural). The space of all trait combinations that are consistent with physical law forms a subset \Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^N. Under typical biological constraints, \Omega may be a finite‑dimensional smooth manifold, a stratified space, or a manifold with corners; minimally, we require \Omega to be a complete separable metric space (Polish space) with a reference distance d_\Omega.

In practice, \Omega is approximated by a morphospace (Mitteroecker & Huttegger, 2009). Discrete genetic variation can be embedded into a continuous ambient space as follows: let genotypes be sequences \mathbf{s}\in\mathcal{A}^K with |\mathcal{A}|=D. Embed alleles into \mathbb{R}^d via a map \hat{\mathbf{q}}:\mathcal{A}\to\mathbb{R}^d; then genotypes are points in \mathbb{R}^{dK}. A smooth phenotype map \mathbf{x}: \mathbb{R}^{dK} \to \mathbb{R}^N is constructed by interpolation such that \mathbf{x}(\hat{\mathbf{q}}(\mathbf{s}))=\hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{s}) for all discrete genotypes. The image of a domain yields a manifold that serves as a local model of \Omega. We emphasise that this continuous embedding is a modelling convenience; the actual constructibility spaces \Omega_G are typically sparse, low‑dimensional subsets determined by developmental bias (see Section 4.3).

3.2 Reference Metric and Dissimilarity

We equip \Omega with a Riemannian metric g_\Omega (where smooth) that encodes physical dissimilarity between organisms. This metric can be chosen as the pullback of the Euclidean metric in trait space, or, more fundamentally, as the Fisher–Rao metric on the manifold of observable probability distributions if organisms are characterised by their observable outputs. The choice is not unique, but the subsequent geometric structures (Wasserstein distances, volumes) depend on it; we therefore treat g_\Omega as part of the modelling hypothesis.

3.3 The Adjacent Possible

The universal space \Omega is vast—Kauffman (2022) estimates the number of imaginable biospheres as 10^{10^{10^6}}—but only a tiny fraction is ever actualised. The adjacent possible (Kauffman, 2000) is the frontier of \Omega accessible from the current state through one step of innovation. Recent formalisations (Rizzo, 2025; Ubaldi et al., 2020) model the adjacent possible as a dynamic network in which nodes are locally stable configurations and edges encode transition probabilities. The constructibility space \Omega_G can be regarded as the subspace of \Omega that is within the adjacent possible of the vehicle–replicator system G.

