5. Metrics on the Space of Genomes

5.1 Fisher–Rao Distance

The geodesic distance induced by h is the Fisher–Rao distance between constructibility measures:

d_F(G_1, G_2) = \inf_{\gamma} \int_0^1 \sqrt{ h_{ab}(\gamma(t)) \dot{\gamma}^a \dot{\gamma}^b } \, dt,

where \gamma is a smooth curve in \mathcal{G} connecting G_1 and G_2. This distance is invariant under reparametrisation of the genome coordinates and captures the minimum information‑theoretic divergence needed to morph one constructibility profile into another.

For genotype spaces embedded as \mathbf{q} \in \mathbb{R}^{dK}, the Fisher metric reduces to the additive genetic covariance matrix (Vanchurin, 2026):

h_{\alpha r, \beta s}(\mathbf{q}) = \frac{\partial x^i}{\partial q^{\alpha r}} G_{ij}(\mathbf{x}) \frac{\partial x^j}{\partial q^{\beta s}},

where G_{ij} is the Fisher information of the phenotype distribution. This is precisely the G‑matrix of quantitative genetics (Lande, 1979). Thus the metric structure is empirically accessible.

5.2 Wasserstein Constructibility Distance

When a common reference metric d_\Omega exists on \Omega, we can compare genomes using optimal transport.

Definition 5.2 (Wasserstein‑2 Distance). For genomes G_1, G_2,

W_2(P_{G_1}, P_{G_2}) = \left( \inf_{\pi \in \Pi(P_{G_1}, P_{G_2})} \int_{\Omega\times\Omega} d_\Omega(\omega_1,\omega_2)^2 \, d\pi(\omega_1,\omega_2) \right)^{1/2},

where \Pi is the set of couplings. If development is deterministic, W_2 reduces to the geodesic distance d_\Omega(\omega_{G_1},\omega_{G_2}).

The Wasserstein distance is sensitive to the metric geometry of the phenotype space. It is not the same as the Fisher–Rao distance; the former measures transport cost, the latter information divergence. The two coincide only in special cases (e.g., Gaussian families with constant covariance). For cross‑species comparisons where the phenotype spaces are not pre‑aligned, one must employ the Gromov‑Wasserstein distance (Mémoli, 2011) or the Hausdorff distance. In practice, tree‑sliced Wasserstein variants (Le et al., 2019) offer computationally tractable approximations.

5.3 Relation to the Shahshahani Metric

In population genetics, the space of allele frequencies on a finite genotype set is a simplex, and evolution under selection and recombination is a gradient flow with respect to the Shahshahani metric (Shahshahani, 1979). Harper (2009) proved that this metric is exactly the Fisher information metric of categorical distributions on the simplex. Our metric h_{ab} is the continuous‑parameter analogue: when the genome space is coarse‑grained to discrete alleles, h reduces to the Shahshahani metric, and the gradient flow becomes the replicator equation.

