7. Geometric Phenotypes: Robustness, Evolvability, and Innovation

7.1 Developmental Robustness

Robustness is the insensitivity of the constructibility profile to genetic or environmental perturbation. In the geometric framework, robustness in the direction of coordinate G^a is measured by the local sensitivity of the probability measure:

R_a(G) := \left\| \frac{\partial P_G}{\partial G^a} \right\|_{W_2} \quad \text{or} \quad R_a(G) := h_{aa}(G)^{-1/2},

where small eigenvalues of h correspond to directions in which P_G changes slowly. The Hausdorff distance d_H(\Omega_G, \Omega_{G+\delta G}) provides a complementary measure of shape robustness.

7.2 Evolvability

Evolvability is the capacity to generate heritable phenotypic variation that is viable and selectable. Within the constructor picture, it is quantified by the volume of the constructibility union within one mutational step:

Definition 7.2 (Local Evolvability). Let \mathcal{R}(G) be the set of genomes accessible from G by a single point mutation. The evolvability is

\mathcal{V}(G) = \operatorname{Vol}_{g_\Omega}\!\left( \bigcup_{G' \in \mathcal{R}(G)} \Omega_{G'} \right).

The Ricci curvature of (\mathcal{G}, h) governs the rate of divergence of geodesics. Negative Ricci curvature—characteristic of many high‑dimensional biological manifolds—accelerates the exploration of novel phenotypes, enhancing evolvability (Ollivier, 2013). By contrast, positive curvature suppresses divergence and promotes canalisation.

7.3 Innovation as Geodesic Crossing

Major evolutionary transitions (e.g., the origin of multicellularity, flight, language) correspond to trajectories that bridge separate basins of the fitness landscape. On the constructor manifold, such transitions are geodesics that cross regions where the fitness gradient vanishes (ridges) to connect disjoint constructibility spaces. The geodesic equation

\frac{D^2 G^a}{dt^2} + \Gamma^a_{bc} \frac{dG^b}{dt} \frac{dG^c}{dt} = 0

gives paths of minimal information distance. The Christoffel symbols \Gamma^a_{bc} encode epistasis and developmental constraints. Innovation occurs when a finite‑size population, driven by drift or biased mutation, manages to follow a geodesic across a fitness valley; the probability of this event is governed by the length of the geodesic and the curvature along it.

The topology of \Omega_G (e.g., its homotopy groups) may obstruct certain evolutionary paths, analogous to topological protection in condensed matter.

