8. Thermodynamics of Construction

8.1 Constructor Reset and Landauer’s Principle

Constructor theory demands that after each performance of a task, the constructor returns to its ready state—for a replicator, this means replication of the digital information. This resetting has an unavoidable thermodynamic cost: Landauer’s principle dictates that erasing one bit of information dissipates at least k_B T \log 2 of heat.

A genome of length L bits, when replicated, does not erase its information but copies it, at a minimal cost of L k_B T \log 2 per copy. The organismal construction itself—development—entails far larger dissipation as the vehicle processes energy and matter. The constructibility capacity \kappa(G) is an entropy; its change during replication is related to the total entropy production \Delta S_{\text{env}} in the environment:

\Delta \kappa \le \frac{\Delta S_{\text{env}}}{k_B}.

This inequality, reminiscent of a fluctuation theorem, suggests that an increase in constructibility capacity (developmental plasticity) must be paid for by increasing thermodynamic dissipation, and conversely, canalisation reduces entropy production. A rigorous derivation of a fluctuation theorem for \kappa(G) remains an open challenge.

8.2 Dissipation During Evolution

As a population evolves, the mean constructibility capacity \bar{\kappa} changes. The gradient flow (2) is dissipative: fitness increases, but the metric itself evolves, potentially altering the information‑theoretic cost of further adaptation. The trade‑off between robustness and evolvability can thus be cast as a thermodynamic constraint: genotypes with high robustness (low h_{aa} in many directions) require larger energetic barriers to leave their canalised states, slowing evolvability.

