9. Extensions: Epigenetic Inheritance and the Adjacent Possible

9.1 Epigenetic Constructors

The digital replicator–vehicle model captures the genetic component of inheritance, but many organisms transmit epigenetic states (DNA methylation, histone modifications, small RNAs) across generations. Lei (2019a,b) has developed a general mathematical framework for heterogeneous stem cell regeneration that includes an epigenetic inheritance kernel p(x,y), the probability that a mother cell in state y produces a daughter in state x. This kernel, often modelled by a Beta distribution, defines an analogue constructor alongside the digital genome.

We extend our framework to the product space \mathcal{G} \times \mathcal{E}, where \mathcal{E} is the epigenetic state space. The combined constructor measure is P_{(G,\epsilon)}, and the metric on the joint space is a product of the Fisher metric from genome variation and an appropriate information metric on epigenetic states (e.g., the Wasserstein metric on the space of histone modification patterns). The FMB law then governs the joint evolution, with the epigenetic kernel contributing to the bias term \mathbf{b}. This unification places gene‑centric and evo‑devo perspectives within a single geometric scheme.

9.2 The Adjacent Possible as a Dynamical Boundary

The constructibility space \Omega_G is a subset of \Omega, but its boundary is not fixed; it expands as the genome explores new regulatory interactions. This expansion can be modelled as the growth of the adjacent possible network. Rizzo (2025) formalised innovation dynamics on Local Optima Networks (LONs), where nodes are locally stable configurations and edges encode transitional probabilities. Walkers on LONs generate Heaps’ law (vocabulary growth), Zipf’s law (frequency distributions), and Taylor’s law (variance scaling) simultaneously—all empirical signatures of evolutionary innovation.

In our framework, the LON can be constructed by coarse‑graining \Omega into basins of attraction of U_G(\omega) and defining edges when a single mutation connects basins. The exploration dynamics then traces a random walk on this network, with the probability of discovering a new constructibility space governed by the network’s degree distribution and the Ricci curvature of the underlying manifold.

