Appendix A: Mathematical Preliminaries

A.1 Riemannian Geometry

Let \mathcal{M} be a smooth finite‑dimensional manifold. A Riemannian metric g is a smoothly varying positive‑definite inner product on each tangent space T_p\mathcal{M}. In local coordinates (x^1,\dots,x^n),

g = g_{ij}(x)\,dx^i\otimes dx^j,
\qquad
g_{ij}=g_{ji},\qquad
g_{ij}(x) \succ 0.

The Christoffel symbols are

\Gamma_{ij}^{k} = \frac{1}{2}g^{k\ell}\bigl(\partial_i g_{j\ell} + \partial_j g_{i\ell} - \partial_\ell g_{ij}\bigr),

where g^{k\ell} is the inverse matrix. A geodesic \gamma(t) satisfies

\frac{d^2\gamma^k}{dt^2} + \Gamma_{ij}^k \frac{d\gamma^i}{dt}\frac{d\gamma^j}{dt} = 0.

The Riemannian volume form is dV_g = \sqrt{\det g_{ij}}\,dx^1\wedge\cdots\wedge dx^n. The Ricci curvature tensor is

R_{ij} = \partial_k \Gamma_{ij}^k - \partial_i \Gamma_{kj}^k + \Gamma_{k\ell}^k \Gamma_{ij}^\ell - \Gamma_{i\ell}^k \Gamma_{kj}^\ell.

A.2 Optimal Transport

Let (\Omega,d) be a Polish metric space. For two Borel probability measures \mu,\nu with finite second moments, the L^2-Wasserstein distance is

W_2(\mu,\nu) = \left( \inf_{\pi\in\Pi(\mu,\nu)} \int_{\Omega\times\Omega} d(x,y)^2\, d\pi(x,y) \right)^{1/2},

where \Pi(\mu,\nu) is the set of couplings (measures on \Omega\times\Omega with marginals \mu,\nu). W_2 metrises the weak-* topology plus convergence of second moments. When \Omega is a Riemannian manifold with metric g_\Omega, the Wasserstein space \mathcal{P}_2(\Omega) is a formal Riemannian manifold; its tangent space at \mu can be identified with gradients of functions, and the W_2 metric obeys the Benamou–Brenier formula:

W_2^2(\mu_0,\mu_1) = \inf_{\rho,v} \int_0^1 \int_\Omega \|v_t(x)\|^2 \,\rho_t(dx)\,dt,

subject to the continuity equation \partial_t\rho_t + \nabla\cdot(\rho_t v_t)=0, \rho_0=\mu_0,\rho_1=\mu_1.

A.3 Information Geometry

A statistical manifold is a parametric family of probability measures \{P_\theta\}_{\theta\in\Theta} on a common sample space \mathcal{X}, with \theta\mapsto P_\theta smooth and injective. The Fisher information metric is

I_{ab}(\theta) = \int_\mathcal{X} \frac{\partial \log p_\theta}{\partial\theta^a} \frac{\partial \log p_\theta}{\partial\theta^b} \, dP_\theta
= -\int_\mathcal{X} \frac{\partial^2 \log p_\theta}{\partial\theta^a\partial\theta^b}\, dP_\theta .

The geodesic distance under I is the Fisher–Rao distance. Chentsov’s theorem (1972) states that any Riemannian metric on the simplex of probability distributions that is invariant under sufficient statistics must be a scalar multiple of the Fisher metric. Thus the Fisher metric is uniquely characterised by the requirement of monotone contractivity under Markov morphisms.

A.4 Shahshahani Metric

On the open probability simplex \Delta_{n} = \{p\in\mathbb{R}^n_+: \sum p_i = 1\}, the Shahshahani metric is

g_{ij}(p) = \frac{\delta_{ij}}{p_i} \quad(\text{with one coordinate eliminated}).

The geodesic distance in this metric is twice the Hellinger arc length. The replicator equation \dot{p}_i = p_i(f_i - \bar{f}) is the gradient flow of mean fitness \bar{f} = \sum p_i f_i with respect to this metric (Harper, 2009).

A.5 Price Equation

Given a population of individuals indexed by i, each with character z_i and fitness w_i (relative contribution to next generation), let \bar{z} = \mathbb{E}[z], \bar{w} = \mathbb{E}[w]. The Price equation is

\Delta\bar{z} = \frac{1}{\bar{w}}\operatorname{Cov}(w,z) + \frac{1}{\bar{w}}\mathbb{E}[w\,\Delta z],

where \Delta z is the within‑individual change (mutation, recombination). This identity holds for any population structure and is the foundation of all selection theory.

