Appendix B: Constructor Theory of Genomes

B.1 Formal Definitions

We follow the constructor‑theoretic framework of Deutsch & Marletto (2014). A substrate is a physical system with a set of disjoint attributes \Sigma. A task \mathcal{T} is a set of ordered pairs \{(x_1,y_1),\dots,(x_k,y_k)\}\subseteq \Sigma\times\Sigma of input–output attributes. The task is possible if there exists a constructor C—an entity that, when presented with the substrate in any input attribute x_i, transforms it to the output attribute y_i and retains the ability to perform the task again (resiliency). Otherwise the task is impossible.

A constructor C is modular if it can be decomposed into a replicator R (a digital information medium) and a vehicle V (a physical machine) such that V uses the information in R to perform the task and, in the process, copies R as part of the output.

Theorem B.1 (Constructor theory of life, Marletto 2015). Under no‑design laws that permit digital information, accurate self‑reproduction requires a modular constructor: a replicator and a vehicle.

B.2 Genome as Constructor

Let S_0 be a standard nutrient bath. Let E be the set of permissible environmental states (temperature, pH, etc.). We model E as a measurable space with a reference probability measure \nu (natural frequency of environments). The genome G is a finite string over a digital alphabet (e.g., \{A,C,G,T\}), physically instantiated as a DNA molecule. The cellular machinery (ribosome, polymerases, maternal gradients, etc.) constitutes the vehicle V.

Definition B.2 (Task \mathcal{T}_G). The task associated with genome G and vehicle V is

\mathcal{T}_G = \big\{ \big((e,S_0), (\omega, G')\big) \,\big|\, e\in E,\; \omega\in\Omega_{G,e},\; G'=G \text{ (faithful copy)} \big\},

where \Omega_{G,e} is the set of macroscopic states (organisms) that arise when G and V develop in environment e. In words: the constructor takes the nutrient substrate and an environmental state, and delivers an organism \omega and a copy of the genome G.

Proposition B.3. Under the no‑design laws of quantum electrodynamics, the task \mathcal{T}_G is possible for a vehicle V that implements translation, metabolism, and replication.

Proof sketch. The existence of the genetic code and the universality of the translation machinery demonstrate that a digital information medium can direct the synthesis of proteins. The possibility of self‑reproduction has been proved in constructor theory (Marletto, 2015) given the existence of a replicator and vehicle. The detailed construction of V is a biological fact; here we assume its possibility as a physical process. ∎

Consequently, for each genome G there exists a well‑defined constructibility space

\Omega_G = \bigcup_{e\in E} \Omega_{G,e},

which is the set of all organisms that can be produced from G by some permitted environment. The collection \{\Omega_G\} forms the constructor family of G.

B.3 Properties

Resiliency. The genome G is copied during each act of construction, so it remains available as a constructor for further tasks. This resiliency is the physical basis of heredity.

Digital nature. The possibility of accurate copying (fidelity \approx 1-10^{-9} per base) follows from the constructor theory of information: a substrate can serve as an information medium only if the copy task is possible with arbitrarily high accuracy.

