Appendix C: Construction of the Universal Space \Omega

C.1 Phenotypic Traits

We describe an organism by a vector \mathbf{x} \in \mathbb{R}^N of measurable phenotypic traits (morphological distances, metabolic rates, etc.). Not every point in \mathbb{R}^N corresponds to a physically viable organism; viability is imposed by constraints:

· Physical constraints: energy conservation, structural stability, mass bounds.
· Biological constraints: developmental accessibility, homeostasis.

Thus \Omega \subset \mathbb{R}^N is defined by a set of inequalities h_k(\mathbf{x})\le 0, k=1,\dots,K, where the h_k are smooth functions.

C.2 Manifold with Corners

Assume that the constraints are regular: the gradients \nabla h_k are linearly independent at the boundary. Then \Omega is a smooth manifold with corners of dimension N. In generic position, \Omega is a finite union of submanifolds stratified by the number of active constraints. Each stratum is a smooth manifold. The strata are glued along boundaries, giving \Omega the structure of a stratified space with a well‑defined length metric.

We equip \Omega with a Riemannian metric g_\Omega on its interior. A natural choice is the pullback of the Euclidean metric on trait space via the embedding, or more fundamentally, the Fisher–Rao metric on observable phenotypes if organisms are characterised by probability distributions of observable features. For quantitative genetics, g_\Omega is often taken as the Euclidean metric (the metric in which the genetic covariance matrix is defined).

C.3 Polish Space Structure

With the induced geodesic distance d_\Omega, \Omega is a complete separable metric space (Polish space). This guarantees that the space of probability measures \mathcal{P}(\Omega) endowed with the Wasserstein distance is again a Polish space (Villani, 2009, Thm 6.18). This fact is essential for the well‑posedness of the Wasserstein constructibility distance.

---

Appendix D: Constructibility Measures and the Fisher Metric

D.1 Developmental Probability Measure

Fix a genome G. The developmental process is a stochastic dynamical system \Phi_G: \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}\times E\times\mathcal{R} \to \Omega, where \mathcal{R} is the space of molecular fluctuations. For each environment e, let \mu_{G,e} be the conditional distribution of the final state \omega = \lim_{t\to\infty}\Phi_G(t,e,\cdot), assuming it exists. The unconditional constructibility measure P_G is the mixture

P_G = \int_E \mu_{G,e} \, d\nu(e),

where \nu is the canonical environmental distribution.

Assumption D.1 (Regularity). The map G\mapsto P_G is smooth (in the sense of Fréchet) from \mathcal{G} (a finite‑dimensional parameter space of genomes) to \mathcal{P}(\Omega) equipped with the topology of weak convergence. Moreover, P_G has a smooth density p_G = dP_G/dV_{g_\Omega} on the interior of its support.

Remark. This assumption is an idealisation. In reality, bifurcations in gene regulation may cause non‑smoothness. Our theory can be extended by working with piecewise smooth families or by employing the Wasserstein distance, which does not require densities.

D.2 Fisher Information on the Constructor Manifold

Given the family \{P_G\}_{G\in\mathcal{G}}, the tangent space is spanned by the score functions \partial_a \ell_G, where \ell_G = \log p_G. The Fisher information metric is

h_{ab}(G) = \int_\Omega (\partial_a \ell_G)(\partial_b \ell_G) \, dP_G
= \mathbb{E}_{P_G}\!\big[\partial_a \ell_G \,\partial_b \ell_G \big].

Theorem D.2 (Chentsov, Petz). The Fisher metric is the unique Riemannian metric (up to scaling) on the statistical manifold that is invariant under sufficient statistics and contracts under Markov transitions. Therefore, any meaningful metric on the space of constructibility measures that respects the information‑processing structure must be the Fisher metric.

D.3 Relation to Kullback–Leibler Divergence

For small parameter differences \delta G,

D_{KL}(P_G \| P_{G+\delta G}) = \frac{1}{2} h_{ab}(G) \delta G^a \delta G^b + O(\|\delta G\|^3).

Thus h is the Hessian of the KL divergence at zero. The geodesic distance under h approximates the square root of the KL divergence for nearby measures.

---

Appendix E: Genetic Covariance as Fisher Metric

E.1 Embedding of Genotypes

Let genotypes be sequences \mathbf{s} \in \mathcal{A}^K with |\mathcal{A}| = D. We embed alleles into \mathbb{R}^d via a one‑hot encoding or a continuous embedding \hat{\mathbf{q}}:\mathcal{A}\to\mathbb{R}^d. Then a genotype \mathbf{s} corresponds to a vector \mathbf{q}(\mathbf{s}) \in \mathbb{R}^{dK}. The phenotype map \mathbf{x}: \mathbb{R}^{dK} \to \mathbb{R}^N is an interpolating function such that \mathbf{x}(\mathbf{q}(\mathbf{s})) = \hat{\mathbf{x}}(\mathbf{s}) for all sequences \mathbf{s}, where \hat{\mathbf{x}} is the measured trait value.

We treat \mathbf{q} as the genome coordinate G = \mathbf{q}. The constructibility density becomes p_{\mathbf{q}}(\mathbf{x}) = p_G(\mathbf{x}), where \mathbf{x} denotes a point in \Omega.

E.2 Fisher Metric in Trait Space

Define the trait Fisher information matrix

G_{ij}(\mathbf{x}) = \mathbb{E}_{\mathbf{x}}\!\big[ \partial_i \log p_{\mathbf{q}}(\mathbf{x}) \, \partial_j \log p_{\mathbf{q}}(\mathbf{x}) \big],

where the derivatives are with respect to the traits. If the phenotype distribution is multivariate Gaussian with mean \boldsymbol{\mu}(\mathbf{q}) and covariance \boldsymbol{\Sigma}(\mathbf{q}), then

h_{ab} = \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{\mu}^T}{\partial q^a} \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{\mu}}{\partial q^b} + \frac{1}{2}\operatorname{tr}\!\left( \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} \frac{\partial\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}{\partial q^a} \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} \frac{\partial\boldsymbol{\Sigma}}{\partial q^b} \right).

The first term dominates when phenotypic variance is largely genetic; this term is exactly the additive genetic covariance matrix \mathbf{G} weighted by the inverse phenotypic covariance.

E.3 Reduction to the G‑Matrix

Assume small genetic variance so that \boldsymbol{\Sigma} is approximately constant across genotypes. Then \partial \boldsymbol{\Sigma}/\partial q \approx 0, and

h_{ab} \approx \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{\mu}^T}{\partial q^a} \boldsymbol{\Sigma}^{-1} \frac{\partial \boldsymbol{\mu}}{\partial q^b}.

In standard quantitative genetics, the additive genetic covariance matrix \mathbf{G} of the traits \mathbf{x} is related to the regression of genotype on phenotype. Under the infinitesimal model, the matrix of genetic covariances equals \sum_{l} 2\sigma_l^2 \mathbf{D}_l\mathbf{D}_l^T, where \mathbf{D}_l = \partial \boldsymbol{\mu}/\partial q^l. Thus, up to scaling by environmental variance, h_{ab} is exactly the G‑matrix, as asserted.

E.4 Shahshahani Metric on Simplex

Consider a single locus with n alleles. The genotype space is the simplex of allele frequencies \mathbf{p}=(p_1,\dots,p_n). The constructibility measure for a diploid population can be defined by the genotype frequencies under random mating. The Fisher information metric for the multinomial distribution of genotypes is

h_{ij} = \frac{\delta_{ij}}{p_i} + \frac{1}{1-\sum p_i} \quad(\text{constant}),

which, after eliminating one coordinate, becomes the Shahshahani metric h_{ij} = \delta_{ij}/p_i. The gradient flow of mean fitness \bar{f} = \sum_{i,j} p_i p_j f_{ij} with respect to this metric yields the classical continuous‑time replicator equation \dot{p}_i = p_i(f_i - \bar{f}), where f_i = \sum_j p_j f_{ij}. This demonstrates the consistency of our geometric framework with classical population genetics.

---

Appendix F: The Force–Metric–Bias Law

F.1 Derivation from the Price Equation

Consider a vector of traits \mathbf{z} (which may include genome coordinates). The Price equation gives

\Delta \bar{\mathbf{z}} = \frac{1}{\bar{w}}\operatorname{Cov}(w,\mathbf{z}) + \frac{1}{\bar{w}}\mathbb{E}[w\,\Delta\mathbf{z}],
\tag{F.1}

where \Delta\mathbf{z} is the within‑generation change (transmission bias: mutation, recombination).

Define the selection differential vector \mathbf{S} = \operatorname{Cov}(w,\mathbf{z})/\bar{w}. In a multivariate quantitative genetic setting, we have the Lande equation \Delta\bar{\mathbf{z}} = \mathbf{G}\boldsymbol{\beta}, where \mathbf{G} = \operatorname{Cov}(\mathbf{z},\mathbf{z}) is the genetic covariance matrix and \boldsymbol{\beta} = \nabla_{\bar{\mathbf{z}}} \log \bar{w} is the selection gradient (Lande, 1979). Adding a bias term \mathbf{b} = \mathbb{E}[w\Delta\mathbf{z}]/\bar{w} gives

\Delta\bar{\mathbf{z}} = \mathbf{G}\boldsymbol{\beta} + \mathbf{b}.
\tag{F.2}

Frank (2025) recognised that this is a universal Force–Metric–Bias structure: \mathbf{G} is a metric tensor converting the force \boldsymbol{\beta} into displacement, \mathbf{b} is a deterministic bias, and stochastic noise \boldsymbol{\xi} can be added to account for drift:

\Delta\bar{\mathbf{z}} = \mathbf{G}\boldsymbol{\beta} + \mathbf{b} + \boldsymbol{\xi}.
\tag{F.3}

F.2 Covariant Form

In geometric terms, the metric \mathbf{G} is the Fisher metric h on the space of genome coordinates. The force is the gradient of expected fitness F(G)=\mathbb{E}[f]. Thus (F.3) becomes

\Delta G^a = h^{ab} \partial_b F \, \Delta t + b^a \Delta t + \xi^a,

where we have introduced a continuous‑time step \Delta t. Taking the limit \Delta t\to 0 yields the stochastic differential equation of gradient flow with bias and noise.

---

Appendix G: Gradient Flow and Classical Dynamical Equations

G.1 Pure Selection Gradient Flow

Assume no bias (\mathbf{b}=0) and no noise (\boldsymbol{\xi}=0). Then the dynamics reduces to

\frac{dG^a}{dt} = h^{ab}(G)\, \partial_b F(G).
\tag{G.1}

This is the Riemannian gradient flow of the fitness function F with respect to the metric h. The rate of fitness increase is

\frac{dF}{dt} = \partial_a F \,\frac{dG^a}{dt}
= \partial_a F \, h^{ab} \partial_b F = \|\nabla_h F\|_h^2 \ge 0,

proving Fisher’s fundamental theorem in its geometric formulation: fitness never decreases under natural selection.

G.2 Reduction to the Multivariate Lande Equation

Project the flow onto the mean phenotype \bar{\mathbf{x}} = \int \mathbf{x} \, p_G(\mathbf{x})\,dV. Using the chain rule and the definition of h,

\frac{d\bar{\mathbf{x}}}{dt} = \mathbf{J} \frac{dG}{dt} = \mathbf{J} h^{-1} \mathbf{J}^T \,\boldsymbol{\beta},

where \mathbf{J} = \partial\bar{\mathbf{x}}/\partial G is the Jacobian of the genotype–phenotype map. The matrix \mathbf{J} h^{-1} \mathbf{J}^T is precisely the additive genetic covariance matrix \mathbf{G} of the traits (see Appendix E). The vector \boldsymbol{\beta} = \nabla_{\bar{\mathbf{x}}} \log \bar{w} is the selection gradient. Thus we recover the Lande equation:

\frac{d\bar{\mathbf{x}}}{dt} = \mathbf{G} \boldsymbol{\beta}.

G.3 Reduction to Replicator Dynamics

Consider a haploid population with discrete alleles. The genome coordinate is the allele frequency vector \mathbf{p} on the simplex. The Fisher metric is the Shahshahani metric h_{ij} = \delta_{ij}/p_i (in non‑redundant coordinates). The expected fitness is F(\mathbf{p}) = \sum_i p_i f_i (assuming constant fitnesses). The gradient flow (G.1) becomes

\dot{p}_i = \sum_j h^{ij} \frac{\partial F}{\partial p_j} = p_i (f_i - \bar{f}),

since h^{ij} = p_i \delta^{ij}. This is the classical replicator equation.

G.4 Mutation–Selection Balance

Including a bias term \mathbf{b} representing mutation: \mathbf{b} = \mathbf{M}\mathbf{p} where \mathbf{M} is a mutation rate matrix. The full dynamics

\dot{p}_i = p_i(f_i - \bar{f}) + \sum_j (\mu_{ji} p_j - \mu_{ij} p_i)

is again a gradient flow with a modified force; the bias can sometimes be absorbed into an effective fitness landscape (the quasi‑species equation).

