Appendix H: Geometric Quantification of Robustness and Evolvability

H.1 Robustness as Metric Flatness

Robustness to a perturbation \delta G is measured by the change in constructibility:

\Delta P \approx \partial_a P_G \, \delta G^a.

In the Wasserstein sense, the magnitude is \| \partial_a P_G \|_{W_2}. This is equivalent to the local Fisher information: for a small coordinate change, \|\partial_a P_G\|_{W_2} \approx \sqrt{h_{aa}(G)}. Thus small eigenvalues of h correspond to robust directions: mutations along these eigenvectors barely alter the constructibility profile. Conversely, large eigenvalues indicate sensitive directions where mutations drastically reshape the distribution.

Robustness can also be expressed in terms of the Hausdorff distance between constructibility spaces:

d_H(\Omega_G, \Omega_{G+\delta G}) = \max\Big\{\sup_{\omega\in\Omega_G} d(\omega,\Omega_{G+\delta G}), \sup_{\omega'\in\Omega_{G+\delta G}} d(\omega',\Omega_G)\Big\}.

If this distance is O(\|\delta G\|^2), the space is locally rigid; if O(\|\delta G\|), it is flexible.

H.2 Evolvability and Ricci Curvature

Define the one‑mutant neighbourhood \mathcal{N}(G) = \{G' : d_H(\text{sequence}, \text{one‑edit}) \le 1\}. The evolvability volume is

\mathcal{V}(G) = \operatorname{Vol}_{g_\Omega}\!\left( \bigcup_{G'\in\mathcal{N}(G)} \Omega_{G'} \right).

On a Riemannian manifold, the growth of volumes of small geodesic balls is controlled by the Ricci curvature: for a ball of radius \varepsilon in direction v,

\operatorname{Vol}(B_\varepsilon) = \varepsilon^n \left(1 - \frac{\operatorname{Ric}(v,v)}{6}\varepsilon^2 + O(\varepsilon^4)\right).

Negative Ricci curvature causes volumes to grow faster than Euclidean, i.e., the space of reachable phenotypes expands more rapidly. Therefore, negative Ricci curvature of (\mathcal{G},h) is a geometric signature of high evolvability.

H.3 Geodesics and Innovation

Evolutionary innovations (major transitions) are modelled as geodesic paths that cross a fitness valley. On a curved manifold, the geodesic deviation equation describes the relative acceleration of nearby paths:

\frac{D^2 J^a}{dt^2} + R^a_{\,bcd} \dot{\gamma}^b \dot{\gamma}^c J^d = 0,

where J is the Jacobi field (deviation vector). Negative sectional curvature amplifies initial deviations exponentially, allowing a population to explore distant regions of \mathcal{G} more efficiently.

When the constructibility spaces of two distant genotypes \Omega_{G_1} and \Omega_{G_2} are topologically distinct (e.g., different homotopy types), any continuous evolutionary trajectory connecting them must pass through a region where the developmental map is singular (a topological phase transition). The order of such a transition is constrained by the topology.

