Appendix I: Thermodynamics of Construction

I.1 Landauer’s Principle and Replication

Landauer’s principle states that erasing one bit of information in a memory at temperature T dissipates at least k_B T \log 2 of heat into the environment. Copying a bit, on the other hand, can be performed reversibly in principle. Replicating a genome of length L bits requires copying L bits; the minimal thermodynamic cost is L k_B T \log 2 if all bits are independently copied without error correction. Error correction (proofreading) incurs additional dissipation proportional to the error rate \eta: roughly L k_B T \log(1/\eta) for a fidelity 1-\eta.

I.2 Constructibility Capacity and Entropy

The constructibility capacity \kappa(G) = e^{H(P_G)} is a measure of the Shannon entropy (in nats) of the developmental distribution. A change in capacity \Delta\kappa corresponds to a change in entropy \Delta H = \log \kappa. The second law of thermodynamics requires that the total entropy production of the constructor plus environment be non‑negative:

\Delta S_{\text{env}} + \Delta H \ge 0.

Hence \Delta\kappa \le \exp(\Delta S_{\text{env}}/k_B), establishing an upper bound on the increase of constructibility capacity in terms of heat dissipation. In a fluctuating environment, a Jarzynski‑type equality may hold:

\mathbb{E}\!\big[ e^{-\Delta S_{\text{env}}/k_B} \big] = 1,

from which one could derive constraints on the distribution of \kappa. A full fluctuation theorem for \kappa remains to be proven but is plausible given the information‑theoretic formulation.

I.3 Constructor Reset

After each act of construction, the vehicle must return to its ready state. This resetting often involves degradation of the previous organism’s biomass and recycling of materials, all of which are highly dissipative. The total free energy required per generation can be expressed as F = T \Delta S_{\text{min}}, where \Delta S_{\text{min}} is the minimum entropy increase to reset the system. This lower bound depends on the complexity of the constructor and places a thermodynamic constraint on the rate of evolution.

---

Appendix J: Epigenetic Inheritance as an Analogue Constructor

J.1 Epigenetic State Space

Let \mathcal{E} be the space of epigenetic states (e.g., histone modification patterns, DNA methylation profiles). This is typically a high‑dimensional continuous space. During cell division, an epigenetic inheritance kernel K(x,y) gives the probability density that a daughter cell has state x given the mother’s state y. For many histone marks, K(x,y) is well approximated by a Beta distribution (Lei, 2019a).

J.2 Joint Constructor

The joint system of genome G and epigenome \epsilon can be regarded as a composite constructor. The constructibility measure becomes P_{(G,\epsilon)}, a measure on \Omega \times \mathcal{E} (or just \Omega if the epigenome is considered part of the organismal phenotype). The epigenetic kernel contributes a stochastic bias to the evolutionary dynamics.

J.3 Product Metric

The space \mathcal{G}\times\mathcal{E} carries a natural product information metric:

h_{(G,\epsilon)} = h_G \oplus h_\mathcal{E},

where h_G is the Fisher metric of the genetic constructors and h_\mathcal{E} is an appropriate information metric on the space of epigenetic states (e.g., the Wasserstein metric on probability distributions of chromatin marks). The gradient flow then has a component driving epigenetic adaptation as well as genetic.

J.4 Unified FMB Law

The force–metric–bias law extended to the joint system reads

\Delta \begin{pmatrix} \bar{G} \\ \bar{\epsilon} \end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix} h_G & 0 \\ 0 & h_\mathcal{E} \end{pmatrix}^{-1}
\begin{pmatrix} \nabla_G F \\ \nabla_\epsilon F \end{pmatrix}
+ \begin{pmatrix} \mathbf{b}_G \\ \mathbf{b}_\epsilon \end{pmatrix} + \boldsymbol{\xi}.

Here \mathbf{b}_\epsilon includes the epigenetic inheritance bias (the mean shift due to the kernel K). This framework unifies genetic and non‑genetic inheritance within the same geometric picture.

