Appendix K: Adjacent Possible and Innovation Dynamics

K.1 Local Optima Networks (LONs)

A Local Optima Network (Rizzo, 2025) is a graph whose nodes are local maxima (or basins) of the fitness landscape. Two nodes are connected if a single mutational step can transition from one basin to another. The adjacency matrix encodes transition probabilities.

K.2 Construction of LON from \Omega_G

For each genome G, we identify the basin of attraction B_G \subseteq \Omega_G as the set of phenotypes that are canalised (high probability under P_G). The basins form a covering of the constructibility space. A LON is built by taking the set of distinct basins \mathcal{B} = \{B_G\} and defining edges B_{G_1} \sim B_{G_2} if there exists a single point mutation from G_1 to G_2 such that \Omega_{G_2} intersects a different basin.

K.3 Exploration Dynamics

A population exploring the LON undergoes a random walk with a drift towards higher fitness. The growth of the number of visited nodes V(t) follows Heaps’ law V(t) \sim t^\gamma with \gamma < 1, and the frequency distribution of visits to nodes follows Zipf’s law f(r) \sim r^{-\alpha}. These empirical laws are reproduced by LON models with realistic topology, providing a testable link between the geometry of constructibility and macroevolutionary patterns.

---

Appendix L: Empirical Measurement Protocols

L.1 Estimating P_G

For model organisms, one can generate isogenic lines and rear them in a range of controlled environments that sample the ensemble E with frequency \nu. High‑throughput phenotyping yields a sample \{\omega_i\} from P_G. Density estimation (kernel methods, normalising flows) then gives an empirical \hat{p}_G. The constructibility capacity \kappa(G) is the exponential of the estimated entropy. The Wasserstein distance between two such measures can be computed via discrete optimal transport (e.g., Sinkhorn algorithm).

L.2 Measuring the G‑Matrix

The additive genetic covariance matrix \mathbf{G} is routinely estimated in quantitative genetics through parent–offspring regression or mixed models (Lynch & Walsh, 1998). In our framework, \mathbf{G} serves as a local approximation of the Fisher metric h. Comparing the empirical G‑matrix with the Fisher information computed from P_G (via density derivatives) provides a consistency test of the theory.

L.3 Geometric Reconstruction of \Omega_G

The constructibility space support can be inferred from the convex hull of phenotyped points, or more accurately through persistent homology to capture topological features. The metric g_\Omega can be taken as Euclidean or, for non‑linear traits, estimated via maximum likelihood embedding (e.g., multidimensional scaling of phenotypic distances). The curvature of \mathcal{G} can then be approximated from the divergence of phenotyped replicates under controlled mutations.

These empirical protocols, though challenging, demonstrate that the framework is in principle falsifiable and open to experimental scrutiny.

